# Video 20 · angle 2 (a stage of life, the first year of motherhood) · afternoon slot · day 7
# If You Became a Mother in 1955, This Was Your World

sc("""If you became a mother in nineteen fifty-five, you were probably very young. The typical American woman married for the first time at about twenty, and had her first baby a year or two later. You might have been twenty-one, holding a newborn, in a small new house, with a husband who left for work at seven in the morning. And you were expected to know exactly what to do.""",
   "1950s young mother baby", "A MOTHER IN 1955")
sc("""You were also part of something enormous. In nineteen fifty-five, about four million babies were born in the United States. Maternity wards were full. New schools were being planned for children who hadn't been born yet. You were one of millions of young women raising the baby boom.""",
   "1950s hospital nursery babies", "4 MILLION BABIES")
sc("""Today, let's follow a young mother through that first year. From the hospital to the nursery, the kitchen, the backyard and the pediatrician's office. If you were a mother in the fifties, or a baby with one, I think much of this will feel very familiar.""",
   "1950s mother baby carriage sidewalk", "THE FIRST YEAR")

ch("The hospital")
sc("""Just a generation earlier, most American babies were born at home. By nineteen fifty-five, almost all were born in a hospital. Women went into the hospital as soon as labor began. Husbands drove them there, kissed them goodbye at the door, and then went to the waiting room. Fathers were almost never allowed in the delivery room.""",
   "1950s hospital maternity ward nurse", "FATHERS IN THE WAITING ROOM")
sc("""In the waiting room, the fathers paced, smoked and read old magazines. When the baby arrived, a nurse came out to announce it. Then the new father handed out cigars to everyone in sight, often with a band that said It's a Boy or It's a Girl. It was a ritual all over America.""",
   "1950s father waiting room cigar", "IT'S A BOY")
sc("""Many mothers were given heavy medication during labor, a method called twilight sleep, and some remembered almost nothing of the birth. Afterward, mothers usually stayed in the hospital for five days to a week. The babies stayed in a nursery behind a big glass window, and were brought to their mothers on a strict schedule.""",
   "1950s hospital nursery window babies", "A WEEK IN THE HOSPITAL")
sc("""Family and friends came to the window to look at the baby, pointing and waving. Grandmothers argued about whose side of the family the baby looked like. And the hospital bill? For a normal delivery and a week's stay, it might be around a hundred to two hundred dollars, often partly paid by the husband's insurance.""",
   "1950s family hospital window newborn", "WHO DOES HE LOOK LIKE?")

ch("Coming home")
sc("""Then you went home. And home, for many young mothers in nineteen fifty-five, was a brand-new house in a brand-new suburb, far from their own mothers and sisters. Their husbands left early and came home at six. For the first time, a young woman might spend long days alone with a baby, with no one to ask for help.""",
   "1950s suburban house new neighborhood", "ALONE ALL DAY")
sc("""But there were neighbors, and they mattered enormously. On streets full of young families, mothers leaned over backyard fences, shared coffee in each other's kitchens, and passed baby clothes from house to house. They watched each other's children. For many women, those neighbors became lifelong friends.""",
   "1950s women neighbors coffee kitchen", "THE NEIGHBORS")
sc("""And there was Dr. Spock. Benjamin Spock's book, The Common Sense Book of Baby and Child Care, first published in nineteen forty-six, was on almost every mother's nightstand. It sold millions of copies. Its famous first line told mothers, trust yourself, you know more than you think you do. For a frightened young mother, those words meant everything.""",
   "1950s mother reading book baby", "DR. SPOCK")
sc("""Then there was the nursery. Many young couples painted a small bedroom pale yellow or soft blue. There was a wooden crib, often a hand-me-down, a changing table and a rocking chair. A mobile hung over the crib. And on the wall, maybe a picture of a lamb or a little Bible verse. It was simple, but it was made with love.""",
   "1950s nursery crib baby room", "THE NURSERY")

ch("The baby's day")
sc("""Babies in the fifties wore cloth diapers, folded and fastened with big safety pins. Every day, the dirty ones went into a pail, and then into the washing machine, and then onto the clothesline. A young mother might wash and hang dozens of diapers a week. Diaper services, which picked up dirty diapers and delivered clean ones, were a luxury many families loved.""",
   "1950s clothesline diapers backyard", "CLOTH DIAPERS")
sc("""Disposable diapers existed, but they were expensive and not very good. Pampers wouldn't arrive in stores until the early sixties. Over the cloth diaper went a pair of rubber pants. And many mothers began toilet training very early, sometimes before the baby's first birthday.""",
   "1950s mother changing baby", "RUBBER PANTS")
sc("""Feeding was different too. In the fifties, breastfeeding had fallen out of fashion. By the late fifties, only about one in four babies was breastfed when they left the hospital. Most mothers used formula, often a homemade mixture of evaporated milk, corn syrup and water, measured out and boiled in glass bottles on the stove.""",
   "1950s mother bottle feeding baby", "EVAPORATED MILK")
sc("""Doctors also recommended feeding babies on a strict schedule, every four hours, whether they seemed hungry or not. Babies started on cereal very early, sometimes within weeks. And jars of Gerber baby food were a modern wonder, lined up on the shelves of every supermarket in strained peas, carrots and applesauce.""",
   "1950s baby food jars supermarket", "EVERY FOUR HOURS")
sc("""Babies slept on their stomachs, which was the advice of the day. They were bundled in flannel and wrapped in hand-knitted blankets from their grandmothers. And when it was warm, mothers often put the baby outside in the carriage for a nap in the fresh air. It was believed to make them strong and healthy.""",
   "1950s baby sleeping carriage outside", "FRESH AIR")

ch("The fear of polio")
sc("""Every mother in nineteen fifty-five carried one big fear. Polio. Every summer, outbreaks swept through American towns, and it mostly struck children. Mothers kept their kids away from swimming pools and crowded places. A child with a stiff neck or a fever could make a mother's heart stop.""",
   "1950s polio children hospital", "THE BIG FEAR")
sc("""Then, on April twelfth, nineteen fifty-five, the news came. Jonas Salk's polio vaccine had been tested on more than a million children, and it worked. Church bells rang. People cried in the streets. Mothers lined up with their children at schools and clinics to get the shots.""",
   "1950s children polio vaccine line", "APRIL 12, 1955")
sc("""For a mother with a new baby that year, it was an enormous relief. Her child might grow up without the fear she had lived with every summer. Within a few years, polio cases in America dropped by about ninety percent. Few things in the fifties made mothers happier.""",
   "1950s mother child doctor office", "AN ENORMOUS RELIEF")
sc("""Doctors were very different then too. The family pediatrician was treated almost like a god. Mothers rarely questioned his advice. He might come to the house when a baby was sick, carrying a black bag. And a visit to his office often ended with a lollipop, and a weight and height written on a little card for the baby book.""",
   "1950s doctor house call baby", "THE PEDIATRICIAN")

ch("The house and the work")
sc("""The baby was only part of the work. In nineteen fifty-five, a mother was also expected to keep a perfect house. Floors swept and mopped, beds made, laundry washed and ironed, dishes done, and a hot dinner on the table when her husband walked in the door. Studies at the time found many housewives worked more than fifty hours a week.""",
   "1950s housewife cleaning house", "50 HOURS A WEEK")
sc("""Ironing alone took hours. Shirts, dresses, sheets, pillowcases and even handkerchiefs were ironed. Before permanent press fabric arrived in the sixties, nearly everything wrinkled. Many women sprinkled clothes with water, rolled them up, and stored them in the refrigerator until they had time to iron.""",
   "1950s woman ironing clothes", "EVEN THE HANDKERCHIEFS")
sc("""There were new machines to help. Automatic washing machines, electric dryers, refrigerators with freezers, and vacuum cleaners. For many young mothers, those machines were a real blessing compared with how their own mothers had lived. But the standards rose with them. The house was supposed to be spotless, all the time.""",
   "1950s woman washing machine laundry", "NEW MACHINES")
sc("""And dinner was serious business. A mother in nineteen fifty-five might cook meatloaf, pot roast, tuna noodle casserole or pork chops, with a vegetable, potatoes and Jell-O salad. Cookbooks like Betty Crocker's showed her how. And the whole family sat down together, every night, usually around six.""",
   "1950s family dinner table", "DINNER AT SIX")
sc("""And then the baby book itself. Many mothers kept one carefully, writing down the first smile, the first tooth, the first word and the first step. They tucked in a lock of hair from the first haircut, and a hospital bracelet. Today, those little books are some of the most precious things a family owns.""",
   "1950s mother writing baby book", "THE BABY BOOK")

ch("Getting out")
sc("""Many young mothers in the suburbs didn't drive, or didn't have a car during the day. The family had one car, and the husband took it to work. So mothers walked. They pushed big baby carriages to the park, to the store, and to each other's houses. A big carriage with a hood and large wheels was a proud possession.""",
   "1950s mother pushing baby carriage", "THE BIG CARRIAGE")
sc("""Groceries were often delivered, or bought once a week when the husband could drive. The milkman came to the door. So did the bread man, the Fuller Brush man, and the Avon lady. For a mother stuck at home with a baby, those visits were a little bit of company in a long, quiet day.""",
   "1950s milkman delivering door", "THE MILKMAN")
sc("""And on Sunday, the whole family went to church, the baby dressed in a little white outfit. Grandparents came for Sunday dinner, or the family drove to visit them. Those Sundays were often the one day a young mother could hand the baby to someone else for an hour and rest.""",
   "1950s family Sunday church baby", "SUNDAY")
sc("""And there were cameras. Many fathers bought a Kodak Brownie, or a home movie camera, just to film the new baby. The prints came back from the drugstore a week later, small and square with white borders. Those home movies, flickering and silent, are now how many of us first saw our own mothers when they were young.""",
   "1950s father home movie camera family", "THE KODAK BROWNIE")

ch("The pressure")
sc("""It wasn't always easy. Magazines and television showed mothers who were always smiling, always calm and always in a pretty dress. Real mothers were tired. Many felt lonely. Some felt they had given up dreams of college or a career, and didn't feel they could say so out loud.""",
   "1950s tired mother kitchen", "ALWAYS SMILING")
sc("""In nineteen sixty-three, Betty Friedan wrote a book called The Feminine Mystique, about what she called the problem that has no name. Many women who had been young mothers in the fifties read it and recognized themselves. But in nineteen fifty-five, there were few words for those feelings, and most mothers simply carried on.""",
   "1960s woman reading book", "THE PROBLEM THAT HAS NO NAME")
sc("""There were also practical limits. In many states, a married woman had trouble getting credit in her own name. Many employers wouldn't hire a mother with small children, and some fired women as soon as they became pregnant. A young mother in nineteen fifty-five depended on her husband in ways her granddaughters can hardly imagine.""",
   "1950s woman bank counter", "DEPENDENT ON HIM")

ch("What they gave")
sc("""And yet, many women who became mothers in the fifties remember those years with deep love. Their children were healthy. Their husbands had steady jobs. Their neighbors were young and full of hope. Many say those years, with little children running through a small house, were the happiest of their lives.""",
   "1950s mother children backyard playing", "THE HAPPIEST YEARS")
sc("""Those mothers raised the largest generation in American history. They packed the lunches, sewed the costumes, ran the PTA, drove the Cub Scouts, and sat up with fevers through the night. The baby boom didn't just happen. Millions of young mothers made it happen, one long day at a time.""",
   "1950s mother packing school lunch", "THEY RAISED A GENERATION")
sc("""If your mother had you in the fifties, think of her for a moment. Twenty-two years old, in a small kitchen, with a baby on her hip and a pot on the stove. She probably never thought she was doing anything special. But she was.""",
   "1950s mother baby kitchen", "THINK OF HER")
sc("""So tell us in the comments. If you were a young mother in the fifties, what do you remember most? Or tell us about your own mother, and what she did for your family. We read every comment. Next time, we're asking what happened to the American drive-in theater. Thank you for spending this time with me.""",
   "1950s family portrait mother", "TELL US ABOUT YOUR MOTHER")

